In this example, we will create a JavaScript function that extracts text from a document. We will use the `Document` object and its methods to achieve this.

### Function Implementation

```javascript
/**
 * Extracts text from a document.
 * 
 * @param {Document} doc - The document object.
 * @returns {string} The extracted text.
 */
function extractTextFromDocument(doc) {
    // Check if the document object is valid
    if (!doc || !(doc instanceof Document)) {
        throw new Error('Invalid document object');
    }

    // Use the all text content of the document
    return doc.documentElement.textContent;
}

// Example usage:
const htmlString = `
    <html>
        <body>
            <h1>Hello World!</h1>
            <p>This is a sample document.</p>
        </body>
    </html>
`;

// Create a new document
const parser = new DOMParser();
const doc = parser.parseFromString(htmlString, 'text/html');

// Extract text from the document
const text = extractTextFromDocument(doc);

// Log the extracted text
console.log(text);
```

### Explanation

*   The function `extractTextFromDocument` takes a `Document` object as an argument and returns the extracted text as a string.
*   It first checks if the provided document object is valid. If not, it throws an error.
*   The `textContent` property of the `documentElement` is used to get the text content of the entire document.

### Note

*   The `DOMParser` API is used to create a new document from an HTML string.
*   The example usage demonstrates how to create a new document from an HTML string and extract text from it.

### Alternative Approach using a Library

If you are working with complex documents or need more advanced text extraction capabilities, consider using a library like `jsdom` or `cheerio`.

